From: Codex <codex@local>
Subject: server: identify poisoned object release caller on PS4

The AFD fd releases return normally, then release_object faults while reading a
pointer filled with Wine's 0x55 uninitialized-memory pattern. Log only that
sentinel pointer and its caller before the existing fault, avoiding a broad
object trace.

---
diff --git a/server/object.c b/server/object.c
index ede1b14a4e..e140de0113 100644
--- a/server/object.c
+++ b/server/object.c
@@ -619,6 +619,15 @@ struct object *grab_object( void *ptr )
 void release_object( void *ptr )
 {
 #ifdef __PROSPERO__
+    if ((uintptr_t)ptr == (uintptr_t)0x5555555555555555ULL)
+    {
+        extern int sceKernelDebugOutText( int channel, const char *text );
+        char line[160];
+        snprintf( line, sizeof(line),
+                  "wineserver: release_object poison ptr=%p caller=%p anchor=%p\n",
+                  ptr, __builtin_return_address(0), (void *)(uintptr_t)&release_object );
+        sceKernelDebugOutText( 0, line );
+    }
     /* ⚠ DIAGNOSTIC. init_directories() passes the result of each create_*()
      * straight to release_object(), assuming none can fail. On this console
      * some do, and the null dereference here is the first anyone hears of it --
